Here is a **clear and simple explanation in natural human language** about **inheritance in functional interfaces** in Java.

---

# Functional Interface + Inheritance (Simple Explanation)

A **functional interface** means an interface that has **only one abstract method**.

When interfaces **inherit from other interfaces**, we must check **how many abstract methods exist in total**.

---

# 1️⃣ Parent has one abstract method, Child has none

If the **parent interface has one abstract method** and the **child interface does not declare any new abstract method**, then the child **still has only one abstract method (inherited)**.

So the **child is still a functional interface**.

### Example

```java
interface Parent {
    void show();
}

interface Child extends Parent {
}
```

✔ Child is **functional interface**
Because it **inherits only one abstract method**.

Simple meaning:

> Parent gives the method to child, so child still has only one abstract method.

---

# 2️⃣ Parent has one abstract method, Child adds another abstract method

If the **child interface adds another abstract method**, then total abstract methods become **two**.

So it **is not a functional interface anymore**.

### Example

```java
interface Parent {
    void show();
}

interface Child extends Parent {
    void display();
}
```

❌ Not a functional interface
Because there are **two abstract methods**.

---

# 3️⃣ Child redeclares the same abstract method

If the child **declares the same abstract method again**, it still counts as **one abstract method**.

So the interface **remains functional**.

### Example

```java
interface Parent {
    void show();
}

interface Child extends Parent {
    void show();
}
```

✔ Still functional interface

Because both methods are **the same method**.

Simple meaning:

> Child is just repeating the same method, not adding a new one.

---

# 4️⃣ Default and Static Methods Don't Affect Functional Interface

Even if the interface contains **default methods or static methods**, it can still be a functional interface.

Because **only abstract methods are counted**.

### Example

```java
interface Test {

    void show();  // abstract method

    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Default method");
    }

    static void print() {
        System.out.println("Static method");
    }
}
```

✔ Still a functional interface.

---

# ⭐ Simple Rule to Remember

Only **abstract methods count**.

| Methods        | Functional Interface? |
| -------------- | --------------------- |
| 1 abstract     | ✔ Yes                 |
| 2 abstract     | ❌ No                  |
| default/static | ✔ Allowed             |

---

# ⭐ Best Interview Explanation

You can say:

> In inheritance, if a child interface inherits one abstract method from the parent and does not add any new abstract method, it is still a functional interface. If the child adds another abstract method, then it is no longer a functional interface. Default and static methods do not affect functional interfaces because only abstract methods are counted.

